The contracts, verified:

```tsx
// PROPS — inherit the native element, add your layer
type ButtonProps = React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<'button'> & {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost';
  size?: 'sm' | 'md';
};
// native props (onClick, disabled, type...) arrive FOR FREE, typed.

// CHILDREN — the renderable union, not ReactElement
type CardProps = { children: React.ReactNode };   // strings, numbers,
// fragments, null — EVERYTHING renderable. ReactElement misses 'hi'.

// EVENTS — generic'd synthetic events
type onChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => void;
//                                  the GENERIC = e.currentTarget's type
function onKey(e: React.KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
  if (e.key === 'Enter') save();      // e.key: string, discriminant-tested
}

// STATE — unions over booleans; the updater's param is typed
const [status, setStatus] = useState<'idle' | 'saving' | 'error'>('idle');
setStatus('saving');                   // typo of 'saveing' = compile error
// DOM refs — null before mount:
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement | null>(null);
useEffect(() => inputRef.current?.focus(), []);

// DISCRIMINATED UNIONS — the crown pattern
type Request =
  | { state: 'idle' }
  | { state: 'loading' }
  | { state: 'error'; message: string; retry: () => void }
  | { state: 'success'; data: User[] };
function View({ req }: { req: Request }) {
  switch (req.state) {
    case 'loading': return <Spinner />;
    case 'error':   return <Error message={req.message} onRetry={req.retry} />;
    case 'success': return <List users={req.data} />;
    // req.message is ONLY accessible in 'error' — the discriminant
    // narrows. IMPOSSIBLE combos (error without message) can't be BUILT.
  }
}
```

Why discriminated unions beat booleans (the argument worth making unprompted): isLoading + error + data as separate fields permits 'loading AND error AND data' — states that can't happen but must be HANDLED. One state: 'idle'|'loading'|'error'|'success' with variant-specific fields makes illegal states UNREPRESENTABLE — the compiler enforces the state machine instead of runtime guards. The same pattern types VARIANT PROPS ({ variant: 'link', href } | { variant: 'button', onClick } — a link without href can't be constructed).

The generic-component layer (one step up): function List<T>({ items, render }: { items: T[]; render: (item: T) => ReactNode }) — the component adapts to its payload; T flows from the items prop. Combined with keyof/constraints, that's the typed-table/select family — the reusable-component story interviews want. And the setup layer: 'use client' components in Next keep all of this; Server Components lose event handlers (no onClick — they can't hydrate) — the props stay typed, the interactions move to client children.
